<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Religious pluralism</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Religious pluralism is the coexistence of several religions within a single society or state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a> was notable for accommodating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and traditional beliefs, which eased commercial relations with diverse partners. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhimmi">dhimmi</a> framework allowed Christians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a> to practise their faiths in exchange for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> tax.</p></div>
